problems.problem:
In a $10\times 10$ square of squared paper one must place one ship $1\times 4$, two ships $1\times 3$, three $1\times 2$ and four $1\times 1$. The ships must not have common points (even vertices) with each other, but they may adjoin the boundary of the square. Prove that

a) if they are placed in the order indicated above (starting with the large ones), this process can always be completed, even if at every moment one cares only about the current ship without thinking about the future ones;

b) if they are placed in the reverse order (starting with the small ones), a situation may arise in which the next ship cannot be placed (give an example).

Geologists took on an expedition $80$ cans of food, whose weights are all known and distinct (there is a list). After some time the labels on the cans became unreadable, and only the quartermaster knows what is where. He can prove this to everybody (that is, justify which can contains what) without opening the cans, using only the preserved list and a two-pan balance with a pointer showing the difference of the weights on the pans. Prove that for this purpose

a) four weighings suffice;

b) three weighings do not suffice.
